120 runs. Three questions, asked of the same data.

1. What is wrong: 7 failures, by population.

   document          4 of 74      5% of that population
   warehouse         2 of 20     10% of that population
   unanswerable      1 of 20      5% of that population

   Sort by rate, not by count. document produces the most failures because it is
   the largest population; warehouse fails at 10%, which is the highest rate and
   therefore the population to work on. It is 20 cases, so a fix aimed
   at it is a fix you can measure — and a fix aimed at 'accuracy' is not.

2. What is slow: the ten slowest, against a median of 3.8s.

   tail-rotated-util       15.4s   9,640 tokens  document
   abstain-19              11.8s   5,784 tokens  unanswerable
   tail-three-months       11.7s   9,404 tokens  document
   abstain-09               9.3s   5,193 tokens  unanswerable
   abstain-12               9.2s   4,544 tokens  unanswerable

   The slowest is 4.1x the median. Tail latency is not an average
   problem: it is a small number of runs doing much more work, and §28.6's
   step budget is the lever that bounds them.

3. What to add to the eval set.

   8 cases earned a place: every failure, every wrong refusal,
   and the three slowest. They are already written — that is the point of
   triaging a run rather than inventing cases at a whiteboard.

     7 failures            they are regressions the moment they are fixed
     4 wrong refusals      the most expensive failure mode to leave in
     3 slowest runs        a latency budget needs cases that test it
     6 counted twice       a wrong refusal is usually also a failure

   And one thing the card does not show: the most expensive answer used 11.2x
   the tokens of the median one (9,640 against 862). A mean cost per
   answer hides that entirely, which is why §28.1 budgets per request rather
   than per month.

That is the loop. Ship, measure, triage the failures into the set, fix the
worst-rate population, re-measure against the card. Every step of it is
evidence, and none of it needs anybody to have an opinion about what users want.
